Reason: 64-bit: GetAtanTable indexes atntable with (int)(ratio*1024+0.5); a NaN ratio gives INT_MIN and a huge one (a denormal side, so __fres is infinite) INT_MAX. With 32-bit addresses (retail, 32-bit build) their element offsets wrap to atntable[0] and to the two bytes before the table, but on a 64-bit host they fault. Wrap the byte offset to a signed 32-bit one there: an unsigned one made INT_MAX 4 GiB past the table (the Puku launch pads in Pinna Park, report f4be7fc1).
--- a/src/MarioUtil/MathUtil.cpp
+++ b/src/MarioUtil/MathUtil.cpp
@@ -128,7 +128,15 @@
 		return atntable[0];
 
 	f32 inv = __fres(param_1);
+#if defined(__SIZEOF_POINTER__) && __SIZEOF_POINTER__ == 8
+	// NaN converts to INT_MIN, a huge ratio to INT_MAX; with 32-bit addresses
+	// their offsets wrap to atntable[0] and atntable[-1] (what retail and the
+	// 32-bit build read), so wrap the byte offset to signed 32 bits here too.
+	s32 offset = (s32)((u32)(int)(param_2 * inv * 1024.0f + 0.5f) * sizeof(u16));
+	return *(const u16*)((const u8*)atntable + offset);
+#else
 	return atntable[(int)(param_2 * inv * 1024.0f + 0.5f)];
+#endif
 }
 
